WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.254
Socket I/O is rarely as simple as one send and one receive.

2
00:00:04.254 --> 00:00:09.162
A read can return fewer bytes than requested, even when the connection is still healthy.

3
00:00:09.162 --> 00:00:12.107
A write can accept only part of a buffer.

4
00:00:12.107 --> 00:00:17.343
Operations can block, time out, be interrupted, fail, or report that the peer closed the connection.

5
00:00:17.343 --> 00:00:22.251
Secure code treats each of these outcomes as expected behavior, not as strange edge cases.

6
00:00:22.251 --> 00:00:24.541
TCP does not preserve application message boundaries.

7
00:00:24.541 --> 00:00:33.376
If a protocol says a message has a header and a body, the application must collect enough bytes for the header, validate the length, then collect the

8
00:00:33.376 --> 00:00:34.685
body within defined limits.

9
00:00:34.685 --> 00:00:41.557
Using whatever bytes happen to be available as if they were a complete message leads to parsing errors and inconsistent state.

10
00:00:41.557 --> 00:00:45.156
The safer design has explicit read states and clear maximum sizes.

11
00:00:45.156 --> 00:00:46.792
Writes need the same care.

12
00:00:46.792 --> 00:00:55.954
A send operation may write only a prefix of the data, so the code must track what remains and decide whether to continue, wait, time out, or close

13
00:00:55.954 --> 00:00:56.609
the connection.

14
00:00:56.609 --> 00:01:02.826
If the program uses nonblocking I/O or an event loop, partial progress must be represented in connection state.

15
00:01:02.826 --> 00:01:08.061
That state should be bounded so slow or stalled peers cannot grow memory use without limit.

16
00:01:08.061 --> 00:01:09.043
Return values matter.

17
00:01:09.043 --> 00:01:15.587
Defensive code checks them, handles disconnects cleanly, avoids using incomplete data, and preserves safe state when an operation stops halfway.

18
00:01:15.587 --> 00:01:21.150
Buffers should have explicit bounds, and conversions from length fields to allocation sizes should be reviewed carefully.

19
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:27.040
Partial I/O handling is not only about correctness; it is a core part of secure network programming.
